==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|ashamed}}, {{m|enm|aschamed}}, from {{inh|en|ang|āsċeamod}}, past participle of {{der|en|ang|āsċeamian|t=to be ashamed}}, equivalent to {{confix|en|a|shame|ed}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈʃeɪmd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ashamed.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Feeling [[shame]] or [[guilt]].
#* {{RQ:Geneva Bible|Isaiah|XLII|17|298|verso=yes|passage=They ſhal be turned backe : they ſhal be greatly '''aſhamed,''' that truſt in grauẽ images, and ſay to the molten images, Ye are our gods.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1618|author=[[w:John Fletcher (playwright)|John Fletcher]]|chapter=The Loyal Subject|title=Fifty Comedies and Tragedies|location=London|publisher=J. Macock|year_published=1679|section=act V, scene vi|page=279|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uqBSAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA279#v=onepage&q&f=false|passage=Good Sir pardon me, / I feel ſufficiently my follies penance, / And am '''aſham’d,''' that ſhame a thouſand ſorrows / Feed on continually, would I had never ſeen her, / Or with a clearer judgement look’d upon her, / She was too good for me, ſo heavenly good Sir, / Nothing but Heaven can love that ſoul ſufficiently, / Where I ſhall ſee her once again.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1859|author=[[w:Horace_Mann|Horace Mann]]|title=Address at Antioch College
|passage=Be '''ashamed''' to die until you have won some victory for humanity.}}

